import{l as e}from"./index-taTbgQ0k.js";var t=e(`Clock`,[[`circle`,{cx:`12`,cy:`12`,r:`10`,key:`1mglay`}],[`polyline`,{points:`12 6 12 12 16 14`,key:`68esgv`}]]),n={updated:`2026-08-05`,courses:[{slug:`aeo-operator`,title:`AEO Operator`,tagline:`Measure, diagnose, act, verify — the whole loop, on a free account.`,metaTitle:`AEO Operator: A Free Course on Running Answer Engine Optimization`,description:`A free, open self-paced course that teaches Answer Engine Optimization as an experimental discipline: build a prompt set, take a baseline, diagnose why AI answers skip you, ship one change, and prove whether it moved. No login to read. No email required.`,h1:`AEO Operator`,summary:`Most AEO advice is untestable. This course teaches the opposite habit: treat every claim about AI answers as something you measure before and after. Seven modules take you from an empty workspace to a verified result — choosing the ten questions that matter, taking a baseline, separating the three reasons AI skips a brand, shipping one grounded change, and then deciding whether the number that moved is signal or noise. Every lesson is free to read without an account. Every lab runs inside the SolCrys free tier.`,ogImage:`/og/learn-aeo-operator.png`,published:`2026-08-05`,updated:`2026-08-05`,provider:`SolCrys`,level:`Beginner to intermediate`,estimatedMinutes:170,audience:[`SEO and content leads whose organic traffic is being absorbed by AI answers`,`Product marketers who need to know how AI describes their category`,`Founders and growth operators doing AEO without a dedicated team`,`Agency strategists building an AEO service line`],outcomes:[`Write a prompt set that represents real buyer decisions rather than keywords`,`Take a defensible baseline and know how much to trust it`,`Separate the three distinct reasons an answer engine skips a brand`,`Turn one diagnosed gap into one shipped, grounded change`,`Decide whether a movement in your numbers is a result or noise`],requirements:[`A free SolCrys account for the labs — no credit card. Reading requires nothing.`,`One brand or product you can make a small content change to`,`About three hours of reading, spread over at least two weeks because verification is slow by nature`],labBudget:{heading:`What the free tier gives you, and how this course spends it`,intro:`The free tier is deliberately small: 10 tracked prompts, 3 manual ChatGPT checks a month, 1 Deep Analysis, 1 Content Audit, 1 workspace, no credit card. That is not a lot — and that is the point. A real AEO experiment needs a baseline, a change, and a confirmation. Three runs is exactly enough to do that once, honestly, and not enough to fool yourself by re-rolling until you like the number.`,rows:[[`Module 1`,`Workspace + Corporate Context`,`No quota`],[`Module 2`,`10 prompts, then Run 1 of 3`,`Run 1/3`],[`Module 4`,`Deep Analysis + Content Audit`,`1 of 1 each`],[`Module 5`,`Ship one change`,`No quota`],[`Module 6`,`Run 2, then Run 3`,`Runs 2/3 and 3/3`]],note:`Quotas reset monthly, so Module 6 is designed to be done in the following month. That is not a workaround — it is how long a content change actually takes to propagate through answer engines.`},faqs:[{question:`Is this course really free?`,answer:`Yes. Every lesson is readable in full without an account, without an email address, and without a paywall. The hands-on labs use the SolCrys free tier, which needs an account but no credit card. We built it this way on purpose: an execution platform that gates its own teaching material is arguing against itself.`},{question:`Do I need to use SolCrys to take this course?`,answer:`No. The concepts — prompt-set design, the three failure modes, the difference between citation and recommendation, run-to-run variance — apply to any AEO stack or to a spreadsheet and a lot of patience. The labs are written against SolCrys because that is the tool we can give you free access to and describe honestly. If you use something else, the labs become a specification you can implement there.`},{question:`Is there a certificate?`,answer:`Not yet. The first version of this course is deliberately just the material and the labs. If enough people finish it and tell us the credential would matter to them, we will add an assessment.`},{question:`How long does it take?`,answer:`About three hours of reading. But the course cannot be completed in three hours, because Module 6 requires you to wait for a content change to propagate through answer engines and then re-measure. Plan on two to four weeks of elapsed time. Any course that promises you a verified AEO result in an afternoon is teaching you to skip the verification.`},{question:`Who is this not for?`,answer:`If you want a list of tactics to apply without measuring them, this is the wrong course — the entire spine here is measurement discipline, and roughly half the material is about not fooling yourself. If you need enterprise-scale rollout across many brands and markets, start here anyway for the concepts, then talk to us about what changes at that scale.`}],modules:[{slug:`orientation`,number:`01`,title:`Orientation`,blurb:`What actually changed, and how to use this course without wasting your three runs.`,lessons:[{slug:`what-changed`,title:`What actually changed about search`,minutes:8,summary:`AI answer engines do not rank pages for a user to choose from. They read sources and produce one answer. That single structural difference is what makes AEO a different job from SEO.`,sections:[{heading:`The shift is from a list to a decision`,body:[`A search engine returns a list and lets the user decide. An answer engine reads a set of sources and makes the decision on the user's behalf, then presents the result as prose. The user often never sees the list at all.`,`This is not a change in ranking mechanics. It is a change in what the surface is for. Optimizing for position on a list you are no longer shown is the wrong objective function. The question is no longer "where do we rank" but "when the engine composes an answer about our category, are we in it, is what it says about us true, and does it recommend us."`,`Those are three separate questions with three separate failure modes, and most teams collapse them into one metric and then wonder why the metric does not move.`]},{heading:`Answer engines cite consensus, not authority`,body:[`The most common wrong intuition carried over from SEO is that a strong domain wins. Answer engines are not primarily rewarding domain strength. They are assembling a picture from multiple sources and favouring what several independent sources agree on.`,`That is why a brand can hold the top organic position for its own category term and still be absent from the AI answer for that same term. The page ranks. The claim is not corroborated anywhere else. The engine has one source saying it and no second source confirming it, so it composes an answer from the sources that do agree with each other.`,`The practical consequence: a large part of AEO work happens off your own domain. Not because owned content stopped mattering — owned content is what converts the click and what an engine reads to learn your facts — but because a claim that exists only on your site reads to an engine as an assertion, not a finding.`]},{heading:`Why this makes AEO an experimental discipline`,body:[`Answer engines are non-deterministic. Ask the same question twice and you can get different sources, a different ordering, and sometimes a different conclusion. The output is a sample from a distribution, not a lookup.`,`This is the single most important fact in this course, and it has an uncomfortable consequence: any single observation of an AI answer is nearly worthless as evidence. You saw one draw. If you change your content and then look once and it improved, you have learned almost nothing, because it might have improved without you.`,`Everything in the remaining six modules is built on this. You will take a baseline across many samples, make one change, and then re-measure — and Module 6 is entirely about the discipline of deciding whether what you observed is a result or a coincidence.`]}],product:null,lab:null,check:[`Can you state the three separate questions an answer engine raises about your brand — presence, accuracy, and recommendation — and why they can move independently?`,`Why can a page rank first on Google and still be absent from the AI answer for the same query?`,`If you look at one ChatGPT answer before a change and one after, what have you actually learned?`],readNext:[`ai-search-is-decision-layer`,`aeo-vs-seo`,`ai-citations-consensus-not-authority`,`ranking-but-not-cited-in-ai`]},{slug:`how-to-use-this-course`,title:`How to use this course`,minutes:5,summary:`The course spends a fixed, small budget of free-tier quota on one honest experiment. Here is the order, and the one mistake that wastes it.`,sections:[{heading:`The shape of each lesson`,body:[`Every lesson has the same four parts: the concept, where it lives in the product, a lab you can actually run, and a short self-check. The concept sections are the part you should read even if you never open a tool. The labs are what turn reading into a skill.`,`At the end of each lesson there is a Read next block pointing into our reference library. Those are the long-form pieces. This course is the spine; those are the depth. If a lesson raises a question it does not answer, the answer is almost always in one of those links.`]},{heading:`The budget, and the one way to waste it`,body:[`The free tier gives you three manual ChatGPT checks a month, one Deep Analysis, one Content Audit, ten tracked prompts, and one workspace. This course spends that budget on exactly one experiment: baseline, change, confirm.`,`The way to waste it is to run a check early out of curiosity, before your prompt set is right. If you burn a run on a prompt set you are about to rewrite, your baseline and your follow-up are measuring different things and the comparison is meaningless. Do not run anything until Module 2 tells you to.`,`This constraint is real, but it is also the lesson. Teams with unlimited runs re-measure until they see a number they like. A three-run budget forces you to decide in advance what you are testing — which is what a real experiment requires anyway.`]},{heading:`Why you cannot finish this in one sitting`,body:[`Modules 1 through 5 can be done in an afternoon. Module 6 cannot, because it asks you to re-measure after a content change, and a content change does not reach answer engines instantly. Depending on the engine, the source, and how often it is recrawled, that takes days to weeks.`,`Quotas reset monthly, which fits this rhythm. Take your baseline and ship your change in month one; verify in month two. If that feels slow, that is accurate information about AEO rather than a limitation of the course.`]}],product:null,lab:{title:`Set up before you start`,requiresAccount:!0,quota:`No quota consumed`,steps:[`Create a free SolCrys account. No credit card is required.`,`Pick one brand or product you can actually make a content change to. If you cannot ship a change, you cannot complete Module 5 or 6.`,`Do not create prompts or run anything yet. Module 2 covers that, and running early wastes the baseline.`],note:`If you are only here to read, skip this. Every lesson is complete without doing the labs.`},check:[`What is the total free-tier budget this course spends, and on what?`,`Why is running a visibility check before Module 2 a mistake rather than just eagerness?`,`Why is a two-week gap between Module 5 and Module 6 a feature rather than a delay?`],readNext:[`testable-geo-playbook`,`workspace-setup-best-practices`]}]},{slug:`ground`,number:`02`,title:`Ground`,blurb:`Before you measure anything, write down what is true about your brand — in a form a machine can use.`,lessons:[{slug:`why-context-comes-first`,title:`Why context comes before measurement`,minutes:7,summary:`You cannot grade an AI answer as right or wrong without a written statement of what is true. Corporate Context is that statement, and it has to exist before the first run.`,sections:[{heading:`Accuracy needs a reference`,body:[`It is easy to measure whether an engine mentioned you. It is much harder, and much more valuable, to measure whether what it said was correct. That second measurement is impossible without a written source of truth to compare against.`,`This is the reason context comes first in this course rather than as a later refinement. A baseline taken without a reference statement can only tell you presence. A baseline taken with one can tell you presence and accuracy, and accuracy is usually where the actionable problem is hiding.`]},{heading:`What belongs in it, and what does not`,body:[`Corporate Context is not marketing copy. It is the factual substrate: what the product does, who it is for, what it costs, what it does not do, how it differs from the named alternatives, and which claims you can substantiate.`,`The instinct to write it persuasively is the main failure. Persuasive copy is full of unfalsifiable claims — "the leading platform," "trusted by teams everywhere" — and an unfalsifiable claim cannot be used to grade an answer. If an engine said it, you could not mark it right or wrong. Write things that could be checked.`,`The second failure is silence. Whatever your context does not mention cannot be graded, so a gap in the document becomes a blind spot in every accuracy measurement that follows. If pricing is not in there, you will never learn that engines are quoting a price you retired two years ago.`],bullets:[`Include: what it is, who it serves, concrete capabilities, pricing structure, named alternatives and honest differences, substantiable proof points`,`Include: the things you are commonly confused with — a same-name company, an adjacent category, a product you sunset`,`Exclude: superlatives, unfalsifiable positioning, anything you would not want an engine to repeat verbatim`]},{heading:`It is organization-level, not per-workspace`,body:[`One practical detail that surprises people: Corporate Context is set at the organization level and shared across every workspace underneath it. It is not a per-workspace field you can vary by campaign.`,`That is deliberate — the whole point is one canonical statement of brand truth rather than several drifting ones — but it means if you manage several distinct brands, they need separate organizations rather than separate workspaces.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`Corporate Context is at the organization level, under your org settings rather than inside a workspace.`,`It is markdown, and it keeps version history — you can see who changed what and when.`,`Workspaces inherit it. Everything downstream — Deep Analysis, Answer Accuracy grading, drafted actions — reads from it.`]},lab:null,check:[`Why can an accuracy measurement not be made without a written context document?`,`Give an example of a sentence that is good marketing copy but useless in Corporate Context, and rewrite it so it is gradeable.`,`What happens to a topic your context is silent about?`],readNext:[`corporate-context-ai-marketing`,`answer-accuracy-coverage-gap`,`grounding-depth-ai-brand-accuracy`,`managed-corporate-context`]},{slug:`write-your-context`,title:`Writing your Corporate Context`,minutes:10,summary:`A working structure for the document, and the test for whether each line earns its place.`,sections:[{heading:`A structure that works`,body:[`There is no required schema, but the documents that produce good grading tend to share a shape. Start with identity and disambiguation, then capability, then commercial facts, then the competitive frame, then proof.`,`Disambiguation deserves more attention than it usually gets. If any company, product, or common phrase shares your name, say so explicitly and say what you are not. Engines conflate similar names constantly, and a single clarifying sentence in your context is the cheapest fix available for a whole class of wrong answers.`],bullets:[`Identity — legal name, product names, what category you are in, what you are commonly confused with`,`Capability — what the product actually does, stated concretely enough to be checked`,`Commercial — pricing structure, tiers, what is free, how someone starts`,`Competitive — the alternatives buyers actually name, and where you genuinely differ`,`Proof — claims you can substantiate, with the substantiation`]},{heading:`The test for every line`,body:[`For each sentence, ask: if an answer engine repeated this to a buyer, could I mark it true or false? If the answer is no, the sentence is positioning, not context. Move it to your website and take it out of here.`,`The second test: is this still true? Context documents rot faster than anything else in marketing because pricing changes, tiers get renamed, features ship and get retired. A context document that says something you retired is worse than one that says nothing, because it will actively teach the wrong fact and you will grade answers against a standard that is itself wrong.`]},{heading:`Length is not the goal, coverage is`,body:[`A long document is not a good document. What matters is whether it covers the topics buyers actually ask about. The fastest way to find the gaps is to look at the questions in your own sales calls, then check whether your context answers each one.`,`You will come back to this. After Module 4 you will have real evidence about which topics engines get wrong about you, and the first fix for many of them is a missing paragraph here.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`Open your organization settings and find the Corporate Context editor.`,`Write in markdown. Headings help — the structure is read, not just the prose.`,`Save. Version history records the change, so you can revert if a rewrite makes grading worse.`]},lab:{title:`Write the first version`,requiresAccount:!0,quota:`No quota consumed`,steps:[`Write a first Corporate Context covering the five areas above. Aim for something you could hand to a new hire on day one.`,`Apply the falsifiability test to every sentence and cut what fails.`,`Add one explicit disambiguation sentence, even if you think nothing shares your name.`,`Save it. It does not need to be finished — you will revise it in Module 4 with real evidence.`],note:`This is the highest-leverage thirty minutes in the course. Everything downstream reads from this document.`},check:[`Which sentences in your draft would you be unable to grade as true or false?`,`Does your context state what you are not, and what you are commonly confused with?`,`Is every commercial fact in it — pricing, tiers, what is free — currently accurate?`],readNext:[`corporate-context-ai-marketing`,`ai-brand-disambiguation-playbook`,`how-ai-describes-your-brand`,`managed-corporate-context`]},{slug:`brand-facts-and-competitors`,title:`Brand facts and the competitive set`,minutes:6,summary:`Naming your real competitors is a measurement decision, not a marketing one — and getting it wrong quietly corrupts every comparison you make afterwards.`,sections:[{heading:`Name the competitors buyers name`,body:[`The competitive set you track should be the one buyers actually mention, not the one your positioning deck aspires to. These are frequently different, and the difference matters because share-of-voice against a set that does not reflect the real consideration set is a number that cannot inform any decision.`,`The quickest way to get this right is to ask a few recent buyers what else they looked at, or read the alternatives your own sales calls surface. Then add anyone an answer engine names when asked about your category, even if you do not consider them a real competitor — if the engine names them to buyers, they are in your consideration set whether you like it or not.`]},{heading:`Brand facts versus Corporate Context`,body:[`Corporate Context is the narrative substrate. Brand facts are the discrete, checkable statements you want available for grounding — the specific numbers, dates, capabilities, and claims.`,`Keep them separate in your head: context teaches an engine who you are, facts give it things it can quote. Facts can be extracted from documents and URLs and then curated, which is usually faster than writing them from scratch, but curate them properly — an extracted fact that is stale or out of context is a wrong answer waiting to be repeated.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`Competitors are configured on the workspace, alongside the brand you are tracking.`,`Brand facts have their own workspace surface, with extraction from an uploaded document or a crawled URL, then manual confirmation.`,`The onboarding checklist tracks whether competitors have been added — it is one of the three setup steps it watches for.`]},lab:{title:`Set the competitive frame`,requiresAccount:!0,quota:`No quota consumed`,steps:[`Create your workspace if you have not already.`,`Add three to five competitors — the ones buyers name, not the ones you wish they named.`,`Optionally seed brand facts by extracting from your own pricing or product page, then confirm each one is current.`],note:`Still no runs. The next module is where the budget starts.`},check:[`Are the competitors you listed the ones buyers name, or the ones your deck names?`,`Is there a company answer engines mention in your category that you left off because you do not respect them as a competitor?`,`What is the difference in role between Corporate Context and a brand fact?`],readNext:[`workspace-setup-best-practices`,`track-where-competitors-get-cited-in-ai`,`ai-share-of-recommendation`]}]},{slug:`measure`,number:`03`,title:`Measure`,blurb:`Ten questions, chosen well, and the first honest baseline.`,lessons:[{slug:`what-a-prompt-set-is`,title:`A prompt set is not a keyword list`,minutes:8,summary:`Keywords are fragments people type. Prompts are decisions people are trying to make. Porting one into the other is the most common way teams get an AEO programme wrong from day one.`,sections:[{heading:`The unit of measurement changed`,body:[`In search, the unit is a query fragment — two or three words, stripped of intent, matched against a corpus. In AI answers, the unit is a question with a decision behind it: which tool should I use, is this vendor credible, what happens if I do this instead.`,`If you export your keyword list and paste it in as prompts, you will measure something, but it will not be the thing that matters. "aeo tools" as a prompt produces a generic listing answer. "Which AEO tool should a five-person marketing team pick if they cannot afford Profound?" produces the answer a real buyer receives — and the two have different sources, different competitors named, and different implications for what you should do next.`]},{heading:`The four kinds of prompt worth tracking`,body:[`A prompt set that only asks one kind of question gives you a one-dimensional picture. Cover the decision journey instead.`,`The recommendation prompts are the ones that hurt when you lose them, because they are the moment the engine picks someone. The accuracy prompts are the ones that hurt when you win them wrongly — being present in an answer that misdescribes you is worse than being absent.`],bullets:[`Category — what is this, how does it work, what should I know. You are competing with explainers here, not vendors.`,`Comparison — X versus Y, alternatives to X. The competitive set becomes visible in these.`,`Recommendation — what should I use, best tool for a situation. This is where the purchase decision happens.`,`Brand — what is your company, is it any good, what does it cost. This is where accuracy problems surface.`]},{heading:`Ten is a real constraint, not a starter allowance`,body:[`Ten prompts is what the free tier tracks, and it is genuinely tight. But a focused ten beats a sprawling hundred for learning, because with ten you will actually read every answer, and reading the answers is where the insight is.`,`Teams with large prompt sets frequently stop reading the responses and start watching only the aggregate score. That is how a programme becomes a dashboard that nobody acts on. Start small enough that you read everything.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`Prompts live on the workspace prompts surface, where you can add them manually or generate a starter set.`,`The generator proposes prompts from your brand and category — useful as raw material, but edit them. A generated set is a starting point, not a decision.`,`Disabling a prompt frees its slot, so a mistake is recoverable without wasting the allowance.`]},lab:null,check:[`Take one keyword from your existing SEO list and rewrite it as a decision a buyer is trying to make.`,`Which of the four prompt types is missing from the set you were planning?`,`Why is a recommendation prompt more commercially loaded than a category prompt?`],readNext:[`ai-search-prompt-set`,`golden-prompt-set-methodology`,`answer-gap-content-brief`,`what-buyers-ask-ai-prompt-pulse`]},{slug:`choose-your-ten`,title:`Choosing your ten prompts`,minutes:9,summary:`A concrete allocation, the sourcing method, and the rule that keeps your baseline comparable to your follow-up.`,sections:[{heading:`An allocation that balances`,body:[`A workable split across ten: three category, three comparison, two recommendation, two brand. That gives you enough of each type to notice a pattern, and it deliberately weights toward the top of the journey where most brands are weakest.`,`Adjust for your situation. If you are in a category where buyers already know they need the thing, drop a category prompt and add a recommendation one. If you suspect an accuracy problem, take a second brand prompt.`]},{heading:`Where to source them`,body:[`The best prompts come from three places, in this order of quality. First, questions real buyers asked you — sales call notes, support tickets, the questions that come up in demos. These are unimpeachable because they actually happened.`,`Second, what the engines themselves surface. Ask an answer engine a broad question about your category and read what follow-up questions it suggests or what sub-topics it volunteers. That is a direct read on how the engine has structured the space.`,`Third, demand data — the aggregate of what people are asking AI in your industry. Useful for coverage, weaker for specificity, because a high-volume generic prompt often has less decision-weight than a low-volume specific one.`]},{heading:`The freeze rule`,body:[`Once you take your baseline, do not change the prompt set until after your verification run in Module 6. This is the rule that makes the whole experiment valid.`,`If you edit a prompt between baseline and follow-up, you cannot attribute a change in the number to your content work, because the question itself changed. This sounds obvious and is violated constantly, usually with the excuse that the prompt was badly worded. If a prompt is badly worded, fix it now — before the baseline — and accept that anything you change afterwards is a new experiment, not a continuation of this one.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`Add your ten prompts on the workspace prompts surface.`,`Review anything the generator produced and rewrite it into an actual buyer question.`,`Read them once end to end before you run anything. This is your last cheap chance to fix wording.`]},lab:{title:`Build and freeze the set`,requiresAccount:!0,quota:`No quota consumed`,steps:[`Write ten prompts, roughly three category, three comparison, two recommendation, two brand.`,`Source at least three of them from questions real buyers actually asked you.`,`Read the full set aloud. Anything that sounds like a keyword rather than a question, rewrite.`,`Stop. Do not run yet — the next lesson spends the first of three runs.`],note:`This is the last no-quota step. Everything after this costs part of your budget.`},check:[`How many of your ten came from something a real buyer actually said?`,`If you had to defend each prompt as representing a real decision, which one would you struggle with?`,`Why does editing a prompt after the baseline invalidate the comparison?`],readNext:[`golden-prompt-set-methodology`,`aeo-prompt-set-lifecycle`,`ai-search-prompt-set`,`what-buyers-ask-ai-prompt-pulse`]},{slug:`your-first-run`,title:`Your first run: taking a baseline`,minutes:8,summary:`What a run actually does, what the first set of numbers means, and the discipline of writing down your prediction before you look.`,sections:[{heading:`What a run is`,body:[`A run puts each of your prompts to the engine, captures the full response, and then parses it: was the brand mentioned, which sources were cited, which competitors appeared, how was the brand characterised. The value is not the score at the top — it is the corpus of actual answers underneath it.`,`On the free tier a run covers ChatGPT. That is one engine of several, and the results will not generalise perfectly to Perplexity or Google's AI surfaces, which favour different sources. Know that limitation rather than forgetting it: your conclusions are about ChatGPT until you measure elsewhere.`]},{heading:`Write your prediction down first`,body:[`Before you trigger the run, write down what you expect. Which prompts do you think you will appear in? Which competitor do you expect to be named most? What do you think the engine will get wrong about you?`,`This takes two minutes and it is the difference between learning something and confirming what you already believed. Without a written prediction, whatever you see will feel roughly like what you expected, because that is how memory works. With one, you get a genuine surprise, and the surprises are where the work is.`]},{heading:`Reading the first result honestly`,body:[`The first number will probably be lower than you hoped. That is normal and is not yet a finding — it is a starting coordinate. A baseline is not a grade. It is the thing you will subtract from later.`,`Resist two temptations. The first is to immediately re-run because the result looked wrong; that spends a second unit of a three-unit budget on a sample you have no reason to distrust. The second is to start fixing things immediately. You have not diagnosed anything yet — Module 4 is where the answers get read properly, and acting before diagnosis is how teams ship changes that address a problem they do not have.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`Trigger a run from the workspace. On free, this is a manual check rather than a scheduled daily one.`,`The runs surface shows the run and its status; results land on the workspace dashboard when it completes.`,`Read individual responses, not just the aggregate. The per-prompt view is where the real information is.`]},lab:{title:`Run 1 of 3 — the baseline`,requiresAccount:!0,quota:`Spends run 1 of 3`,steps:[`Write your prediction down somewhere outside the tool — three lines is enough.`,`Trigger the run.`,`When it completes, read every response in full before looking at any aggregate number.`,`Note the three things that most surprised you against your prediction.`,`Do not change anything yet.`],note:`If the run surfaces an obviously broken prompt, resist rewriting it. Note it, finish the experiment, fix it in the next cycle.`},check:[`Did you write a prediction before looking? What did you get wrong?`,`Which engine does this baseline actually describe, and what does that mean for how far you can generalise?`,`Why is re-running immediately a bad use of the budget?`],readNext:[`visibility-measurement-methodology`,`how-many-runs-until-ai-visibility-trustworthy`,`ai-brand-visibility-monitoring`,`why-ai-visibility-score-moves`]}]},{slug:`read`,number:`04`,title:`Read`,blurb:`Turning a wall of answers into a picture of who is shaping them.`,lessons:[{slug:`reading-visibility`,title:`Reading presence without over-reading it`,minutes:7,summary:`Presence, share of voice, and how a brand can be mentioned everywhere and recommended nowhere.`,sections:[{heading:`Three numbers that are not the same`,body:[`Presence is whether you appeared at all. Share of voice is how much of the named competitive set was you. Recommendation is whether the engine actually endorsed you rather than merely listing you.`,`These decouple constantly. A brand can appear in nine of ten answers and be recommended in none, because it keeps being named in the roll-call of alternatives while a competitor is named as the choice. On a presence metric that looks like a win. Commercially it is a loss, and a loss you will not notice unless you look at recommendation separately.`]},{heading:`Aggregate scores hide the thing you need`,body:[`A single visibility score is useful for tracking direction over time and nearly useless for deciding what to do. The decision information lives in the per-prompt breakdown: which specific questions you lose, and to whom.`,`Sort by the prompts where you are absent and read those responses first. That set is your actual work queue. A prompt where you are absent and a well-corroborated competitor is recommended is a different problem from one where the engine gave a generic answer naming nobody — the first is a competitive gap, the second is an unclaimed space, and they need opposite responses.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`The workspace dashboard carries the aggregate view — presence, share of voice, movement.`,`The per-prompt view breaks this down by question, which is where you should spend your reading time.`,`Recommendation is scored separately from presence, per response, so a brand that is present but never endorsed shows up as exactly that.`]},lab:{title:`Build your work queue`,requiresAccount:!0,quota:`No quota consumed`,steps:[`List the prompts where you were absent.`,`For each, note whether a competitor was recommended or the answer named nobody in particular.`,`Separate the list into competitive gaps and unclaimed spaces. You will treat them differently in Module 5.`]},check:[`Can you name a prompt where you are present but not recommended, and explain why that is worse than it looks?`,`What is the difference in required response between a competitive gap and an unclaimed space?`,`Why is an aggregate score a poor basis for deciding what to do next?`],readNext:[`ai-share-of-recommendation`,`ai-recommendation-score`,`ai-citation-vs-recommendation`,`is-ai-visibility-a-vanity-metric`]},{slug:`reading-citations`,title:`Reading the citations`,minutes:8,summary:`The list of sources an engine cited is the most actionable artefact you have, because it names exactly which pages you would need to be on.`,sections:[{heading:`Citations are a map of what to influence`,body:[`When an engine composes an answer about your category, it draws on specific URLs. That list is not trivia. It is a directory of the pages that currently shape what buyers are told, and therefore the concrete targets for any off-domain work.`,`Read them as a set, not individually. Look for the pages that recur across many prompts — those are the load-bearing sources in your category, and presence on one of them is worth more than presence on a dozen one-off mentions.`]},{heading:`Cited does not mean the page mentions you`,body:[`A subtle and important distinction: an engine can cite a page that has nothing to say about your brand. The citation tells you the engine used that page to answer the question, not that the page endorsed you.`,`So a page cited on a prompt you lose is a target — being covered there could change the answer. A page cited on a prompt you win, which does mention you, is an asset — worth protecting and worth understanding, because whatever it says about you is currently working.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`The citations surface lists source URLs from your responses, filterable by whether the response mentioned your brand.`,`The mentions-you filter is the fast way to separate assets from targets.`,`Sources are classified by type, which the next lesson uses.`]},lab:{title:`Separate assets from targets`,requiresAccount:!0,quota:`No quota consumed`,steps:[`List the ten most frequently cited URLs across your responses.`,`Mark each as an asset (mentions you favourably), a target (does not mention you but shapes a prompt you lose), or neutral.`,`Note which single target appears across the most prompts. That is your highest-leverage off-domain opportunity.`]},check:[`Which single source URL appears across the most of your prompts?`,`Do you have any assets — cited pages that already describe you well?`,`Why does a citation not imply the cited page mentions your brand?`],readNext:[`ai-answer-citations`,`citation-gap-audit`,`how-to-get-cited-by-chatgpt-2026`,`what-gets-cited-geo-study`]},{slug:`the-source-layer`,title:`The source layer: owned, editorial, community`,minutes:8,summary:`Different source types behave differently, respond to different work, and cannot substitute for each other.`,sections:[{heading:`Four layers, four different jobs`,body:[`Sort your cited domains into owned (yours), competitor, editorial (publications, analysts, reviewers), and community (forums, discussion sites, user-generated content). Each behaves differently and each is influenced by a different kind of work.`,`Owned is the layer you control completely and the layer engines trust least on its own, because a claim on your own site is an assertion. Editorial is slow to move and heavily weighted. Community is fast, volatile, and frequently the largest single share of citations in a technical or software category — and it is also where the most brand-damaging misinformation lives.`]},{heading:`Owned content is not dismissed, it is misunderstood`,body:[`It is common to see the owned share of citations reported as a small percentage and concluded from that that owned content does not matter. That is a misreading. Any single domain holding a meaningful share of a category's citations is a strong position, because the denominator is every source on the open web.`,`More importantly, the layers do different jobs in one funnel. Third-party sources are what make an engine willing to say your name. Your own pages are what the engine reads to get your facts right, and what converts the buyer who clicks through. Trying to substitute one for the other is how programmes end up either invisible or unable to convert the visibility they earned.`]},{heading:`Match the work to the layer`,body:[`The layer distribution in your citations tells you where the work is. If editorial dominates your category, your gap is a coverage and relationships problem. If community dominates, your gap is a participation problem, and the only ethical way in is to be genuinely useful in those communities rather than to plant mentions.`,`If your own domain is absent even from prompts about your own brand, you have a technical or content problem on your site, which Module 4 will help you localise.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`Cited domains are classified by source type, and the classification can be overridden where the automatic call is wrong.`,`Filtering citations by source type gives you the layer distribution for your category.`,`The distribution is worth recording now — it is a slow-moving structural fact you will want to compare against later.`]},lab:{title:`Map your source layer`,requiresAccount:!0,quota:`No quota consumed`,steps:[`Classify your top cited domains into owned, competitor, editorial, and community.`,`Note which layer dominates in your category.`,`Write one sentence naming the kind of work that layer responds to. That sentence constrains what Module 5 can realistically be for you.`]},check:[`Which source layer dominates in your category, and what kind of work does it respond to?`,`Why is it a misreading to conclude from a small owned-citation share that owned content does not matter?`,`If community sources dominate your category, what is the only durable way in?`],readNext:[`ai-source-incentive-map`,`owned-earned-community-sources-ai`,`how-to-build-a-source-layer-strategy`,`reddit-g2-community-sources-aeo`,`wikipedia-techradar-reddit-dominate-ai-citations`]}]},{slug:`diagnose`,number:`05`,title:`Diagnose`,blurb:`Three distinct reasons an engine skips you — and why the fix for each is different.`,lessons:[{slug:`three-failure-modes`,title:`The three failure modes`,minutes:9,summary:`Not retrieved, retrieved but not cited, cited but not recommended. Almost every AEO problem is one of these, and treating them as one problem is why most fixes fail.`,sections:[{heading:`Why one label is not enough`,body:[`"We are not visible in AI" is a symptom, not a diagnosis, and it covers three separate mechanical failures that happen at different stages and need opposite responses. Shipping a fix without knowing which one you have is guessing.`,`The three are sequential. Retrieval has to succeed before citation is possible; citation has to happen before recommendation is on the table. Find the earliest stage that is failing and fix that, because fixing a later stage while an earlier one is broken changes nothing.`],bullets:[`Not retrieved — the engine never reached your content. Crawler access, rendering, or simply no content on the topic.`,`Retrieved but not cited — the engine reached it and did not use it. Usually a corroboration or answer-shape problem.`,`Cited but not recommended — the engine used you as a source and still endorsed someone else.`]},{heading:`Localising which one you have`,body:[`Not retrieved shows up as total absence — your domain appears nowhere in the citations, including on brand prompts where you should be the obvious source. Check crawler access first; content that requires JavaScript to render, or a robots configuration that excludes AI crawlers, produces exactly this pattern.`,`Retrieved but not cited looks like your domain appearing occasionally but never on the prompts you care about. The content exists and is reachable but is not what the engine wants for that question — usually because it does not answer the question in a directly extractable form, or because nothing else on the web corroborates the claim.`,`Cited but not recommended is the most advanced problem and the most commercially frustrating. Your content is good enough to inform the answer and something else is winning the endorsement — typically because the competitor has independent corroboration you lack.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`Crawler access has a dedicated surface — check this first if you suspect a retrieval problem.`,`The citations view with the owned filter tells you whether your domain is being retrieved at all.`,`Recommendation is scored per response, separately from presence, which is how you see the third failure mode distinctly.`]},lab:{title:`Name your failure mode`,requiresAccount:!0,quota:`No quota consumed`,steps:[`Check whether your domain appears at all in your citations.`,`If it does not, check crawler access before anything else.`,`If it appears but not on your priority prompts, you are in mode two.`,`If it appears on prompts where a competitor is still recommended, you are in mode three.`,`Write down which mode you are in. The rest of the course assumes you know.`]},check:[`Which of the three modes are you in, and what is your evidence?`,`Why is it pointless to work on corroboration if you are actually in the retrieval failure mode?`,`What pattern in the citations distinguishes mode one from mode two?`],readNext:[`re-run-diagnosis-three-failure-modes`,`ranking-but-not-cited-in-ai`,`ai-crawler-answer-readiness`,`ai-citation-vs-recommendation`]},{slug:`deep-analysis`,title:`Running a Deep Analysis`,minutes:7,summary:`You have one on the free tier. Spend it on the prompt that matters most, not the one that annoys you most.`,sections:[{heading:`What it adds over reading the response`,body:[`Reading responses tells you what happened. A Deep Analysis works on why — examining the answer against your context and the cited sources to produce reasoning about the gap and specific recommendations rather than a score.`,`The output is only as good as the Corporate Context underneath it. This is where the work in Module 2 pays off, and where a thin context document produces disappointingly generic recommendations. If the analysis comes back vague, the usual cause is that your context did not give it enough to reason with.`]},{heading:`Choosing which prompt to spend it on`,body:[`You have one. The instinct is to spend it on the answer that is most wrong about you, because that one is irritating. Usually the better choice is the prompt with the most commercial weight where you are absent — a recommendation prompt in your core category.`,`Ask which prompt, if the answer changed, would most change the pipeline. Spend the analysis there.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`Deep Analysis is triggered per prompt from the prompt view.`,`Free includes one per month. Recommendations appear under the analysis on the prompt page.`,`Recommendations can be promoted into the Actions queue, which is where Module 5 picks them up.`]},lab:{title:`Spend your one Deep Analysis`,requiresAccount:!0,quota:`Spends 1 of 1 Deep Analysis`,steps:[`Pick the highest commercial-weight prompt where you are absent or losing.`,`Run the Deep Analysis on it.`,`Read the reasoning before the recommendations — the reasoning is what teaches you the pattern.`,`Promote the one recommendation you can actually ship into your Actions queue.`],note:`If the output feels generic, that is diagnostic: go back and thicken your Corporate Context on that topic.`},check:[`Why did you choose that prompt over the one that is most wrong about you?`,`Was the reasoning specific to your brand, or generic? What does that tell you about your context document?`,`Which single recommendation can you actually ship this week?`],readNext:[`citation-gap-audit`,`answer-gap-content-brief`,`inside-the-action-hub`,`ai-visibility-dashboard-vs-aeo-execution-engine`]},{slug:`content-audit`,title:`Auditing one page`,minutes:8,summary:`The page-level view: whether a specific URL is answer-ready, and what recovering the lost points actually requires.`,sections:[{heading:`Page-level readiness is a separate question`,body:[`Prompt-level measurement tells you what engines say about your category. A content audit asks a narrower question about one URL: is this page structured so an engine can extract an answer from it, and is it credible enough to be worth citing.`,`These are graded across content quality, credibility, technical readability, and discoverability. The credibility weighting surprises people — sourced statistics, external citations, and clear authorship carry real weight, which is the page-level expression of the consensus principle from Module 1.`]},{heading:`Choosing the URL`,body:[`You have one audit. Spend it on the page that should be winning a prompt you are losing — not your homepage, which is rarely the page an engine wants for a specific question.`,`If your Deep Analysis pointed at a content gap, the right URL is the page that ought to cover that gap. If no such page exists, that is itself the finding, and Module 5 becomes about creating it rather than fixing something.`]},{heading:`Read the recoverable points, not the score`,body:[`The score is a summary. The useful part is the itemised list of what is missing and how many points each would recover, because that is a ranked work list with effort implied.`,`Work top-down and be honest about which items you can substantiate. An audit may note that sourced statistics would help; inventing a statistic to satisfy it is worse than leaving the points on the table, because a fabricated fact is exactly what accuracy monitoring exists to catch.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`Content Audit runs from the workspace audit surface against a URL you specify.`,`Free includes one per month. There is also a standalone free audit at app.solcrys.com/audit, which is email-gated but does not need a workspace.`,`The report itemises checks with recoverable points and concrete before-and-after guidance.`]},lab:{title:`Audit the page that should be winning`,requiresAccount:!0,quota:`Spends 1 of 1 Content Audit`,steps:[`Choose the URL that should be answering a prompt you are losing.`,`Run the audit.`,`Rank the findings by recoverable points against the effort to fix.`,`Cross off anything you cannot substantiate honestly.`,`Keep the top three. That is your Module 5 scope.`],note:`If no page exists for the gap, note that instead — creating the page is a legitimate and often better Module 5 outcome.`},check:[`Which page did you audit, and why that one rather than the homepage?`,`Which findings did you cross off because you could not substantiate them honestly?`,`What are the top three items you will actually ship?`],readNext:[`solcrys-content-audit-report`,`schema-markup-for-ai-search`,`how-to-write-content-that-gets-cited-by-ai`,`ai-crawler-answer-readiness`]},{slug:`accuracy-and-recommendation`,title:`When presence is not the problem`,minutes:7,summary:`Two failure states that a presence metric will never show you: the engine describes you wrongly, or describes you correctly and still picks someone else.`,sections:[{heading:`Being present and wrong`,body:[`An answer that mentions you and misdescribes you is worse than one that omits you, because it actively teaches a buyer something false and it does so with the engine's authority behind it. Presence metrics score this as a win.`,`Grading requires the reference document from Module 2, which is why context came first. The frequent finding is not dramatic hallucination but quiet staleness — a retired price, a feature you sunset, a positioning you moved away from two years ago. The web still says it, so the engine still says it.`,`Note the coverage limit: anything your context is silent about cannot be graded. If accuracy grading comes back clean, check whether that means you are accurate or whether it means your context does not cover the topics where you are wrong.`]},{heading:`Being present, correct, and still not chosen`,body:[`The hardest state. The engine has your facts right, cites your page, and recommends a competitor anyway. There is nothing to correct.`,`This is almost always corroboration. The competitor's claim is echoed across independent sources; yours exists on your own site and nowhere else. The engine is doing what Module 1 described — favouring what several sources agree on — and the fix is not a better page. It is a third-party source that says the same thing.`,`That is slower and less controllable than editing your own content, which is why teams avoid diagnosing it correctly and ship another landing page instead. If you are in this state, Module 5 has to be about the source layer, not about your own site.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`Answer Accuracy grades responses against your Corporate Context and flags divergences.`,`The grounding view shows what the grade was based on, which is how you tell a real pass from a coverage gap.`,`Recommendation is scored per response, so you can find responses that are accurate and still unfavourable.`]},lab:null,check:[`Is there anything an engine says about you that is stale rather than false?`,`If accuracy grading looks clean, how would you tell whether that is real or a coverage gap?`,`If you are cited, accurate, and still not recommended, what does that tell you the fix has to be?`],readNext:[`is-ai-telling-the-truth-about-your-brand`,`answer-accuracy-coverage-gap`,`fix-wrong-fact-in-ai-answer`,`ai-recommendation-score`,`how-ai-describes-your-brand`]}]},{slug:`act`,number:`06`,title:`Act`,blurb:`One change, chosen to match your diagnosis, shipped in a way you can attribute later.`,lessons:[{slug:`one-change`,title:`Why you ship exactly one change`,minutes:7,summary:`Shipping five changes at once feels productive and destroys your ability to learn anything from the result.`,sections:[{heading:`Attribution requires restraint`,body:[`If you fix the page, publish two new articles, get a review site listing, and update your schema in the same week, and the number moves, you have learned that something worked. You have not learned what, so you cannot do it again on purpose.`,`One change per cycle is slower per week and much faster per lesson learned. After three cycles the team that changed one thing at a time knows three things. The team that changed fifteen things knows that their numbers went up.`,`This is genuinely hard to hold to under pressure to show activity. The defence is to be explicit that the cycle is an experiment with a stated hypothesis, not a sprint of content output.`]},{heading:`Write the hypothesis before you ship`,body:[`State it in one sentence: because the engine is failing at [mode], I am changing [thing], and I expect [specific prompt] to show [specific movement] by [date].`,`A hypothesis that cannot fail is not one. "This will improve our AI visibility" is unfalsifiable. "This should make us appear in the answer to prompt 4, which we currently lose to a named competitor" can be checked, and can be wrong, which is what makes it worth testing.`,`Write down the date you will re-measure, and make it at least two weeks out.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`The Actions queue holds promoted recommendations and tracks state from open through shipped.`,`Keeping the action in the queue rather than in your head is what makes the later comparison possible — it timestamps the change.`,`Record your hypothesis on the action so the next person to look knows what was being tested.`]},lab:{title:`Choose and record one change`,requiresAccount:!0,quota:`No quota consumed`,steps:[`Pick one action, matched to the failure mode you named in Module 5.`,`Write the hypothesis in the one-sentence form above.`,`Set the date you will re-measure, at least two weeks out.`,`Deliberately do not ship the other things on your list this cycle.`]},check:[`Can your hypothesis be wrong? If not, rewrite it until it can.`,`Does the change you picked match the failure mode you diagnosed, or the one that is easiest to fix?`,`What are you deliberately not doing this cycle?`],readNext:[`inside-the-action-hub`,`testable-geo-playbook`,`aeo-actions-to-revenue`]},{slug:`fix-the-page`,title:`Fixing a page so an engine can use it`,minutes:9,summary:`For retrieval and citation failures, what actually changes an outcome — and the shortcuts that produce nothing.`,sections:[{heading:`Answer the question in an extractable form`,body:[`The most reliable page-level improvement is unglamorous: state the direct answer to the question, early, in plain declarative sentences, before the context and the narrative.`,`Engines extract. A page that buries its answer under four hundred words of preamble is harder to extract from than one that answers in the first paragraph and then elaborates. This is not about writing shallowly; elaborate all you want afterwards. It is about not making the extractable claim conditional on reading the whole piece.`,`Define your terms explicitly, use headings that match how the question is actually asked, and put the specific claim, number, or comparison in prose rather than only in an image or a chart.`]},{heading:`Credibility carries real weight`,body:[`Sourced statistics, outbound citations to primary sources, clear authorship, and a visible date all contribute. This is the page-level version of the consensus principle: a page that shows its work reads as more citable than one that asserts.`,`The constraint is honesty. Do not manufacture a statistic to satisfy a checklist, do not invent a review rating, do not add schema describing things that are not on the page. Structured data that does not match the content is a well-known way to get ignored, and a fabricated fact is the thing accuracy monitoring exists to catch.`]},{heading:`What structured data does and does not do`,body:[`Schema helps a machine parse what a page is and how its parts relate. It does not make a page authoritative, and it will not rescue content that has nothing to say.`,`Treat it as removing friction rather than adding weight. Match the schema type to the actual content, keep it complete, and make sure the page is readable without JavaScript — a page whose content only exists after client-side rendering is a retrieval failure waiting to be diagnosed.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`Work from the audit findings you ranked in Module 5.`,`The report gives current-versus-update guidance per check, which is the change specification.`,`Mark the action shipped once the change is live, so the timestamp is recorded.`]},lab:{title:`Ship it`,requiresAccount:!1,quota:`No quota consumed`,steps:[`Make the change on the page and publish it.`,`Confirm the content is visible in the raw HTML, not only after JavaScript runs.`,`Confirm AI crawlers are not excluded from the URL.`,`Mark the action as shipped and note the date.`],note:`If your diagnosis was mode three — cited but not recommended — this lesson is not your fix. Go to the next one.`},check:[`Does your page answer the question in the first paragraph, in prose?`,`Is every claim you added substantiable?`,`Is the content present in the HTML before JavaScript runs?`],readNext:[`how-to-write-content-that-gets-cited-by-ai`,`schema-markup-for-ai-search`,`ai-crawler-answer-readiness`,`solcrys-content-audit-report`]},{slug:`earn-a-source`,title:`When the fix is off your own site`,minutes:8,summary:`For corroboration failures, the work is on someone else's domain — which changes the timeline, the tactics, and the ethics.`,sections:[{heading:`You cannot edit your way out of a corroboration gap`,body:[`If you are cited, accurate, and still not recommended, the missing ingredient is an independent source that says what you say. No amount of work on your own site produces that.`,`The targets are already in your data. Module 4 asked you to identify the source URLs that recur across the prompts you lose. Those are not abstract; they are the specific pages where being covered would change the answer.`]},{heading:`Match the tactic to the layer`,body:[`Editorial coverage responds to being genuinely newsworthy or genuinely useful to that publication's audience — a real finding, a dataset, a point of view worth quoting. It is slow and it does not respond to volume.`,`Review and directory listings are the most mechanical option and often underrated, because they are structured, frequently recrawled, and explicitly comparative.`,`Community sources are the fastest-moving and the most dangerous to approach cynically. The only durable strategy is to be a useful participant. Astroturfing is detectable by humans and increasingly by platforms, and it converts a citation opportunity into a reputational liability.`]},{heading:`Expect a slower clock`,body:[`Off-domain work has a longer lag than a page edit. Publication, then crawl, then incorporation into what engines draw on. If your change this cycle is off-domain, extend your re-measurement date accordingly rather than concluding it failed at two weeks.`,`That is a real cost, and it is why diagnosing correctly matters. Spending a slow cycle on corroboration when your actual problem was crawler access is an expensive mistake.`]}],product:null,lab:{title:`Open one off-domain path`,requiresAccount:!1,quota:`No quota consumed`,steps:[`Take the single most-cited target URL from your Module 4 lab.`,`Identify what would make you legitimately includable there.`,`Take the first concrete step — submit the listing, send the pitch, or start participating properly in the community.`,`Extend your re-measurement date to reflect the slower clock.`],note:`Do not attempt to manufacture community mentions. It is detectable, it is against most platform rules, and it damages the thing you are trying to build.`},check:[`Which specific source, if it covered you, would most change your answers?`,`What would legitimately make you includable there?`,`How much longer than a page edit should you wait before re-measuring?`],readNext:[`how-to-build-a-source-layer-strategy`,`earn-llm-citations-content-source-match`,`reddit-g2-community-sources-aeo`,`are-saas-directory-submissions-worth-it-2026`,`aeo-for-pr`]}]},{slug:`verify`,number:`07`,title:`Verify`,blurb:`The module that separates an AEO practice from a collection of opinions.`,lessons:[{slug:`why-one-run-proves-nothing`,title:`Why one run proves nothing`,minutes:8,summary:`Answer engines are non-deterministic. Understanding what that does to your evidence is the core skill this course exists to teach.`,sections:[{heading:`The number moves on its own`,body:[`Ask the same prompt twice with nothing changed and you can get different sources, a different set of competitors named, and sometimes a different recommendation. The output is a draw from a distribution.`,`This means your measurement has natural variance independent of anything you do. If you observe a change after shipping something, part of that change is your work and part is the distribution. With a single before-and-after observation you cannot tell which part is which — and the honest answer is often that you cannot tell at all.`]},{heading:`How this goes wrong in practice`,body:[`The failure is not usually dishonesty, it is asymmetric attention. A team ships a change, sees the number rise, and stops looking. The same team ships a change, sees the number fall, and concludes the measurement is noisy. Both readings might be right, but applying them selectively guarantees the wrong conclusion.`,`The defence is deciding in advance. Before you re-measure, write down what movement you would count as success and what you would count as failure. Then hold yourself to it even when you dislike the answer.`]},{heading:`Some prompts are noisier than others`,body:[`Variance is not uniform. Some questions have a settled answer that the engines return consistently; others are contested, with the named set shifting between draws.`,`This distinction is directly useful. A settled prompt where you lose is expensive to move but the win is durable. A contested prompt is cheaper to move and less stable — and it is also where an opening still exists, because the consensus has not formed yet. Knowing which of your prompts are which tells you where effort compounds.`]}],product:null,lab:null,check:[`If your number moves after a change, what are the two possible explanations?`,`What movement would you count as failure? Write it down before you look.`,`Which of your prompts do you expect to be settled, and which contested?`],readNext:[`how-many-runs-until-ai-visibility-trustworthy`,`why-ai-visibility-score-moves`,`contested-vs-settled-ai-citation-queries`,`ai-citation-variance-spend-decision`,`testable-geo-playbook`]},{slug:`second-run`,title:`Your second run`,minutes:7,summary:`Re-measure against a frozen prompt set, and read the per-prompt movement rather than the headline.`,sections:[{heading:`Conditions for a valid comparison`,body:[`Three things must be true. The prompt set is unchanged from the baseline. Enough time has passed for the change to propagate — at least two weeks for a page edit, longer for anything off-domain. And you have written down in advance what would count as success.`,`If any is false, run anyway if you like, but be clear that you are collecting an observation rather than testing a hypothesis.`]},{heading:`Read the prompt you targeted first`,body:[`Your hypothesis named a specific prompt. Look at that one before the aggregate, because the aggregate blends the prompt you targeted with nine you did not touch, and that blend can easily hide or manufacture the effect.`,`Then read the actual response, not just whether the flag flipped. Sometimes the informative result is that you are now cited but still not recommended — which is not failure, it is progress to the next failure mode, and it tells you what the following cycle is about.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this lives in SolCrys`,steps:[`Trigger the run the same way as the baseline.`,`The runs surface lets you compare against the previous run.`,`Read the targeted prompt's response in full before looking at deltas.`]},lab:{title:`Run 2 of 3`,requiresAccount:!0,quota:`Spends run 2 of 3`,steps:[`Confirm the prompt set is unchanged and your waiting period has elapsed.`,`Re-read the success criterion you wrote before shipping.`,`Trigger the run.`,`Read the targeted prompt first, in full, then the aggregate.`,`Record what happened without interpreting it yet.`],note:`If quotas reset before you are ready, wait. A premature second run is a wasted third of your budget.`},check:[`Was the prompt set genuinely unchanged?`,`Did the prompt you targeted move, or only the aggregate?`,`Did you move from one failure mode to the next rather than to success?`],readNext:[`why-ai-visibility-score-moves`,`re-run-diagnosis-three-failure-modes`,`visibility-measurement-methodology`]},{slug:`signal-or-noise`,title:`Signal or noise: the third run`,minutes:8,summary:`The run most teams never take, and the only one that tells you whether the second one meant anything.`,sections:[{heading:`What the third run is for`,body:[`The second run gives you one post-change observation. The third gives you a second one, with nothing changed in between — which lets you see how much the number moves on its own.`,`If run three sits close to run two and both are clearly different from the baseline, you have a result worth acting on. If run three swings back toward the baseline, what you saw in run two was substantially variance, and the honest conclusion is that the change did not demonstrably work.`,`This is the run that gets skipped, because by run two the team already has the story it wants. Taking it is the single practice that most separates an AEO programme that compounds from one that accumulates unverified folklore.`]},{heading:`Recording the outcome honestly`,body:[`Write down which of three things happened: the change worked, it did not, or the result was inconclusive. Inconclusive is a legitimate and common outcome, and recording it as such is what stops it from being remembered as a success six months later.`,`Record the effect size too, not just the direction. A change that moved one prompt from absent to mentioned is a different fact from one that moved you from mentioned to recommended across four prompts, and treating them as the same is how a tactic gets over-generalised.`]},{heading:`What to do with each outcome`,body:[`If it worked: do the same class of change on the next-highest-value gap. You have evidence about your specific category, which is worth more than any generic best practice.`,`If it did not: revisit the diagnosis rather than the tactic. The usual cause is a correct fix applied to the wrong failure mode.`,`If it was inconclusive: either the effect is real but smaller than your variance, or there is no effect. Both point to the same next step — pick a higher-leverage change rather than repeating this one harder.`]}],product:null,lab:{title:`Run 3 of 3`,requiresAccount:!0,quota:`Spends run 3 of 3`,steps:[`Change nothing since run two.`,`Trigger the run.`,`Compare all three: baseline, post-change, and confirmation.`,`Write one paragraph stating worked, did not work, or inconclusive — and the effect size.`,`Keep that paragraph. It is the first entry in your own evidence base.`],note:`If the honest answer is inconclusive, write inconclusive. That record is worth more than a flattering one.`},check:[`Did run three confirm run two, or pull back toward the baseline?`,`What effect size did you actually observe?`,`Which of the three next steps does your outcome point to?`],readNext:[`how-many-runs-until-ai-visibility-trustworthy`,`testable-geo-playbook`,`is-ai-visibility-a-vanity-metric`,`why-two-aeo-platforms-can-disagree`]},{slug:`operating-cadence`,title:`Turning this into an operating cadence`,minutes:8,summary:`What the loop looks like when it runs every month, what to report, and what to stop doing.`,sections:[{heading:`The cycle, repeated`,body:[`You have now run the loop once. The practice is that same loop on a monthly cadence: re-measure, diagnose the largest remaining gap, ship one change matched to its failure mode, verify, record.`,`The prompt set should stay stable for several cycles so movement stays comparable, then be revised deliberately rather than continuously — when your category shifts or when a prompt has been settled in your favour long enough to stop being informative. Do not delete prompts that show zero presence. A prompt you have never won is where the opportunity is; deleting it only improves the average.`]},{heading:`What to report upward`,body:[`Report the experiment, not the dashboard. What did we believe, what did we change, what happened, what do we now know that we did not know last month.`,`That framing survives contact with a sceptical executive far better than a visibility score, because it is falsifiable and it accumulates. It also protects you when a month produces an inconclusive result — you still learned something, and you have the record to prove the process is working even when the number did not move.`,`Connect it to the commercial question when you can. Presence on a recommendation prompt in your core category is worth more than presence on a category-definition prompt, and saying so keeps the conversation about pipeline rather than about a number.`]},{heading:`What to stop doing`,body:[`Stop reading single answers as evidence. Stop shipping bundles of changes and attributing the result to whichever one you liked best. Stop reporting the aggregate score as the outcome. Stop deleting the prompts you lose.`,`And stop treating advice — including everything in this course — as true without testing it in your own category. The point of building a measurement loop is that you no longer have to take anyone's word for it, including ours.`]}],product:{heading:`Where this goes next in SolCrys`,steps:[`Paid tiers add scheduled daily tracking and more engines, which shrinks the variance problem by giving you many more samples.`,`The MCP server exposes your workspace to agents, so the monthly read can be drafted programmatically rather than by hand.`,`More Deep Analyses and Content Audits per month means the diagnosis step stops being rationed to one.`]},lab:{title:`Schedule the next cycle`,requiresAccount:!1,quota:`No quota consumed`,steps:[`Put the next cycle in the calendar, one month out.`,`Write down the gap you will target next and why it is the highest-value one remaining.`,`Start a running log of experiments — hypothesis, change, outcome, effect size. Three entries in, it will be more useful than any external best-practice list.`]},check:[`What is the highest-value gap remaining, and what failure mode is it?`,`Can you state last cycle's result in the what-we-believed, what-we-changed, what-we-learned form?`,`Which of the four stop-doings is your team most guilty of?`],readNext:[`aeo-prompt-set-lifecycle`,`aeo-roi-business-case`,`aeo-actions-to-revenue`,`cmo-aeo-procurement-playbook`,`solcrys-mcp-and-skills`]}]}]}]},r=n.courses;n.updated;var i=e=>r.find(t=>t.slug===e),a=e=>{let t=[];for(let n of e.modules)for(let e of n.lessons)t.push({module:n,lesson:e,position:t.length+1});return t},o=(e,t,n)=>`/learn/${e}/${t}/${n}/`,s=e=>`/learn/${e}/`,c=(e,t,n)=>{let r=a(e),i=r.findIndex(e=>e.module.slug===t&&e.lesson.slug===n);if(i!==-1)return{flat:r[i],prev:i>0?r[i-1]:null,next:i<r.length-1?r[i+1]:null}},l=e=>e.modules.reduce((e,t)=>e+t.lessons.length,0);export{i as a,t as c,a as i,r as n,o,c as r,l as s,s as t};